{"hash":"573fe7695e2af00e","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":73.409,"said":"Look along this shore. This land is Prabhasa, and it is where the Mahabharata’s story of the Yadavas comes to its end. The epic tells how Krishna’s clan came on pilgrimage to the Prabhasa sea and, in a dark hour, turned on one another. Balarama left his body by the ocean. Then Krishna lay down in deep yoga, and a hunter named Jara, mistaking him for a deer, shot an arrow into his heel. When Jara ran up and saw what he had done, he was terrified, but Krishna comforted him, and then rose, the epic says, filling the whole sky with splendour. The epic itself names no exact spot. But the people of Prabhas have long kept these memories in two places: Bhalka Tirth, about four kilometres away, where a peepal tree is honoured as the tree Krishna rested under, and Dehotsarg, on the Hiran river close by, remembered as the place of his departure. If you go there, go gently. This is the place where a god forgave the hand that wounded him.\n\nDid you know? In the Mahabharata, Krishna’s first act after being struck by the arrow was to comfort the hunter who had shot it.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":939},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":955,"end":1069}],"words":[[0.098,0.252,0,4],[0.362,0.373,5,10],[0.747,0.168,11,15],[0.927,0.397,16,21],[2.212,0.3,23,27],[2.524,0.276,28,32],[2.812,0.12,33,35],[2.945,0.673,36,44],[3.834,0.18,46,49],[4.026,0.108,50,52],[4.147,0.132,53,55],[4.291,0.192,56,61],[4.495,0.084,62,65],[4.591,0.685,66,79],[5.288,0.349,80,85],[5.649,0.108,86,88],[5.769,0.096,89,92],[5.877,0.541,93,100],[6.43,0.325,101,106],[6.767,0.144,107,109],[6.923,0.204,110,113],[7.139,0.325,114,117],[8.365,0.168,119,122],[8.546,0.3,123,127],[8.858,0.349,128,133],[9.219,0.204,134,137],[9.435,0.553,138,147],[10.0,0.337,148,152],[10.349,0.264,153,157],[10.625,0.156,158,160],[10.793,0.589,161,171],[11.394,0.096,172,174],[11.502,0.084,175,178],[11.599,0.589,179,187],[12.2,0.288,188,19